A||||17||VISCERAL||Anyone turbo a 3800 Series II ?||||1||
Z||000000||VISCERAL||03-26-2001||11:22 AM||||How do you 3800 II owners out there like your motor. Better than a 3.4 huh?<BR>How much power can I expect out of a 3.8 turbo? 300? Stock 205 hp + turbo & intercooler----power?||||reg||1
Z||000001||c m west||03-26-2001||11:54 AM||||We have installed a 3800 Series II motor with a Garrett T3 turbocharger into an 88 Beretta - 450 horsepower at the flywheel, next week we will be installing the same setup into an 88 GT Fiero<P>Eric - West Coast Fiero <A HREF="http://www.cwestco.com" TARGET=_blank>www.cwestco.com</A> <BR>310-305-4111<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000002||gabriel||03-26-2001||01:27 PM||||Exactly how do we go about reaching 450 hp with the 3800 in a Fiero? And still use it as a street car?  Are you going to post pics here or on your site?  Would love to see them before I finish mine.... I have the SC mounted.||||reg||1
Z||000003||c m west||03-26-2001||03:52 PM||||we have not yet completed the car customer has decided to rip out the interior and customized it, and I would rather not show a picture on my site of a destroyed car withy a great looking engine bay. For smog legality we had to retain the automatic transmission for the motor. However as far as horsepower, it is a fully intercooled with 17 lbs of boost utilizing a T3 - I can't wait to finally start on the project next week that will involve this same setup into an 88 Fiero<P>Eric - West Coast Fiero <A HREF="http://www.cwestco.com" TARGET=_blank>www.cwestco.com</A> <BR>310-305-4111<P>||||reg||1
Z||000004||gabriel||03-27-2001||01:29 PM||||&gt;, it is a fully intercooled with 17 lbs of boost utilizing a T3<P>Then, is it so that the T3 will give better results than the SC on the Series II?<P>Intercooled air2air or H20? 17lbs of boost sounds killer! Did you make the intercooler? Can you keep the engine together under that pressure??<P>I'm also using the auto w/a shift kit from TEC.||||reg||1
Z||000005||c m west||03-27-2001||02:13 PM||||turbos ALWAYS give berrer results due to the fact that they acheive free horsepower by utilyzing exhaust gases, instead of drawing away from the motor via a belt - not to mention that a supercharger is always running. The 3800 Series II is as internally sound as a big block, incredibly huge crank journals, rod journals, etc. for it's size, more than capable of handling 17 lbs of boost. The intercooler is an air to air that we fabricated here in house. For more details on this setup give us a call here at the shop - you want to speak with Chris.<P>Eric - West Coast Fiero <A HREF="http://www.cwestco.com" TARGET=_blank>www.cwestco.com</A> <BR>310-305-4111<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000006||Sillhouette.com||03-27-2001||10:33 PM||||Turbos do not give better results they are more efficient than superchargers.  You will be able to run around any turbo setup with a good supercharger.  Go look at the funny car and alcohol drag races you see very big blowers on top of those engines.  <P>Another point your turbo setup is topped off at 17psi.  That is it no more.  With a supercharger you virtually have limitless psi (depending on the supercharger).<P>While turbos are impressive the 3800 turbo has been done nothing new.  GNX.  Why not just use that engine and pump up the pressure to 34psi.  I have seen  that on a track.  <P>Turbos in the rear engine car require a lot of care.  The ultimate weakness of the turbo is heat and guess what you have a lot of heat in the rear of the Fiero.  Plus not to mention the complex oil and coolant lines that give just more possibility for things to go wrong.<P>I know why you like turbos.  They are easier to install than supercharger.  While you only have to change the code of the 2732 between the 1400 - 3400 rpm becasue that is the only places that you have boost.  The supercharger has to be changed from the 400 rpm to the top of the power band 5500 rpm.  <P>Your turbo setup just changes the stock power curve.<P>My supercharger setup gives a completely new power curve.  <P>Later||||reg||1
Z||000007||c m west||03-27-2001||10:56 PM||||any more than 17lbs the motor would lose DAILY drivability and/or the motor would burst<P>Turbo setups are only as complicated as the car they are being installed on<P>Ferarri and Lamborgini have never had a problem with a turbo in the rear, let alone 2<P>The GNX motor is one HEAVY block and would be more effort than worth while as a swap<P>and... turbos just happen to be what we specialize in<P>Eric - West Coast Fiero <A HREF="http://www.cwestco.com" TARGET=_blank>www.cwestco.com</A> <BR>310-305-4111<P>||||reg||1
Z||000008||Standard||03-28-2001||12:12 AM||||dude... you have to run oil and/or coolant lines to a supercharger, too.  Superchargers produce heat...  Anything that compresses air will produce heat.<P>Funny cars etc use blowers because they are limited by the NHRA rulebook.  <P>Turbos are alot more adaptable than superchargers, and actually run cooler than superchargers at high boost (20psi+).  And they are not topped off at 17psi.  You can get more boost out of a turbo than you ever could out of a supercharger, and you wouldn't be sapping any power from the engine to do it.  The s/c on the 3800 series II saps 40 hp(if not more) and they're only running 8(?) pounds of boost, not quite sure of the exact number but I know it isn't much.  And you can blow your engine up just as easily with a s/c as you can with a turbo..  <P>btw.. changing the stock power curve gives you a whole new power curve....  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/rolleyes.gif"><P>||||reg||1
Z||000009||Sillhouette.com||03-28-2001||07:49 AM||||Nope you are wrong.  The supercharger setup does not need coolant lines.  Only oil lines.  <P>By changing stock power curves I mean that you will start at the same place as your stock car since at idle the turbo does nothing than you end up at the same area at the end of your power curve. You will just have a higher hump in the middle.<BR>The supercharger starts at a completly different area than stock and ends up at a completly different area.  It is nothing like the stock curve. <BR>One can get the same results out of a supercharger as out of a turbo at a much lower PSI.  The Rotrax running at 9psi on a STOCK motor gave you 80HP at the wheels (conservatevly) the 17psi of boost will give you about the same using and engine that was designed for boost.  See the difference?<P>As far as heat.  The supercharger pressure pipes are so cool that after running for hours you can actually touch them without burning your hands.  The supercharger runs much cooler than the turbo.  <P>Later||||reg||1
Z||000010||Predator||03-28-2001||08:10 AM||||I'm staying out of the turbo vs blower thing. It has been beat to death here already. c m west, where are you going to place the intercooler for the turbo on the Fiero? I have one for mine, but I cannot figure out a good place to put it. I don't want to commit to a place until I am sure it is the best place for air flow without choping my engine compartment apart. If you can when the setup is finished please post a picture and where you are placing the intercooler. The setup sounds killer. <P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Fiero4_1.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000011||mrfixit58||03-28-2001||09:45 AM||||There are three things guaranteed in life... Death, Taxes, And Sillhouette.com arguing that supercharges are better than turbo.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>Oil IS a coolant. yes, I realize it's a lubricant but without is, friction (heat) will kill the bearing / bushings.<P>The power curve for a supercharge changes over "almost" the entire RPM range. At idle, it produces very little boost. But, after idle boost is almost immediant and continuous.<P>A turbo CAN create power from just off idle is sized to do so. A turbo CAN create boost over ALMOST the entire usuable RPM range if sized properly.<P>Yes, turbos may require "more care" if you consider regular oil changes and the cool down time to prevent oil coking on the bearings "more care".<P>Superchargers are great for drag racing and general power increases. So are turbos. The advantage turbos have in road racing is that they can be designed to produce peak power over EITHER a wide or narrow RPM range. And, the boost can be adjusted DURING operation with the twist of a knob.<P>The NHRA will penalize a car in a particular class if running a turbo because they consider it an "unfair advantage" against supercharged and normally asperated motors. Hummm...<P>Yes, turbos run hotter... because they are driven by EXHAUST gases!<P>Lastly, this is commentary is not intended to sway anyones opinion, only to clearify some vauge points in the discussion. Heck, not even the REAL experts can agree on this topic.<P>Roy  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P><BR>||||reg||11
Z||000012||Sillhouette.com||03-28-2001||10:09 AM||||I have not started this . It was C M West.  His statement above is clear.  "Turbos give always better result...."  <P>All I was doing is pointing out that they do not.  There is little difference between a turbo and a supercharger.  <P>They give the same results so the only thing left to do is talk about reliability..<P>Which is more reliable in a rear engine car.  A supercharger that does not produce any heat or a turbo that does?  <P>I don't car either way I have seen both in a fiero and I have compared both.  I think that for the fiero the supercharger gives better results than a turbo.  <P>The key words I think and FIERO.  <P>Later||||reg||1
Z||000013||Standard||03-28-2001||10:22 AM||||Ummm... again... <B>Anything that compresses air produces heat</B>.  It's physics, plain and simple.  No way to get around it...||||reg||1
Z||000014||stimpy||03-28-2001||12:40 PM||||Plus, who in the hell would want to get rid of their A/C in southern California? Man, I get so tired of these pissing contests. OK, Silhouette wins. Everyone else, pack up your sh!t and go home, there is nothing left to be done as far as Fieros go. Throw away your a/c, bolt on a blower, and call it a day.||||reg||1
Z||000015||Black88GT||03-28-2001||02:09 PM||||Early Dodge Vipers didn't have A/C, didn't see too many people complaining about that.  When you car is faster then 90% things on the road, there are more important things then A/C.<P>just my 2 cents...<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000016||mrfixit58||03-28-2001||02:38 PM||||And... Just how many early Dodge Vipers have YOU seen in stop&go traffic lately?  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"> Dang... where did I put that sunscreen?!?!<P>Roy  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||6
Z||000017||fyerstarter||03-28-2001||03:17 PM||||Turbochargers are more widely used on smaller engines because they do not have too much horsepower in the first place to spare to drive the blower. On a drag car you have a shitload of horses so losing 50 or so to drive a blower and gain a couple hundred is not a big deal. But if you put a blower on a 150 - 200 hp its kinda like taking 2 steps forward and 1 step back. Where as a turbo just uses the wasted exhaust gases which would not be used anyway. Course that is at the expense of more back pressure till the turbo spools up causing turbo lag. So I guess that both are not without their good and bads. I'm just jealous cuz I wish that I had either on my Fiero :P||||reg||1
